{"name":"Harem Silk","hex":"#006383","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"harem","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The private section of a Muslim household forbidden to male strangers. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A group of someone's girlfriends, wives and/or concubines in a polygamous household."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/harem"},{"term":"silk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fine fiber excreted by the silkworm or other arthropod (such as a spider).","A thread or yarn made from silk fibers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To develop silk.","To remove the silk from (corn)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Harem","blocks":[{"text":"A harem or harim is a domestic space that is reserved for the women of the house, especially in a Muslim family. A harem may house a man's wife or wives, their pre-pubescent male sons, unmarried daughters, female domestic servants, and other unmarried female relatives. In the past, during the era of slavery in the Muslim world, harems also housed enslaved concubines. In former times, some harems were guarded by eunuchs who were allowed inside. The structure of the harem and the extent of monogamy or polygyny have varied depending on the family's personalities, socio-economic status, and local customs. Similar institutions have been common in other Mediterranean and West Asian civilizations, especially among royal and upper-class families, and the term is sometimes used in other contexts. In traditional Persian residential architecture, the women's quarters were known as andaruni, and in the Indian subcontinent as zenana."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Afghan_lady_in_Kabul.jpg/330px-Afghan_lady_in_Kabul.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harem","wikidata":"Q165853"},{"title":"Silk","blocks":[{"text":"Silk is a natural protein fiber, some forms of which can be woven or knitted into textiles. The protein fiber of silk is composed mainly of fibroin (75-80%) and a smaller coating of sericin (20-25%). It is most commonly produced from the cocoons of certain insect larvae. The best-known silk is obtained from the cocoons of the larvae of the mulberry silkworm Bombyx mori, which are reared in captivity (sericulture)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Silk_raw_01a.jpg/330px-Silk_raw_01a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silk","wikidata":"Q37681"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/4_New_entrant_to_a_princes_harem._Jaipur%2C_late_18_century%2C_National_Museum_New_Delhi_%282%29.jpg/500px-4_New_entrant_to_a_princes_harem._Jaipur%2C_late_18_century%2C_National_Museum_New_Delhi_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/4_New_entrant_to_a_princes_harem._Jaipur%2C_late_18_century%2C_National_Museum_New_Delhi_%282%29.jpg/1280px-4_New_entrant_to_a_princes_harem._Jaipur%2C_late_18_century%2C_National_Museum_New_Delhi_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/4_New_entrant_to_a_princes_harem._Jaipur%2C_late_18_century%2C_National_Museum_New_Delhi_%282%29.jpg/500px-4_New_entrant_to_a_princes_harem._Jaipur%2C_late_18_century%2C_National_Museum_New_Delhi_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"New entrant to a prince's harem. Jaipur, late 18 century, National Museum, New Delhi","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A4_New_entrant_to_a_princes_harem._Jaipur%2C_late_18_century%2C_National_Museum_New_Delhi_(2).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia